~ The GC must count the memory a list grows into. A list's item array grows with realloc,
~ outside gc_alloc; when that memory was not counted, a loop that kept building big
~ throwaway lists never reached the collection threshold, so nothing was ever freed.
~ Measures the interpreter's peak memory (VmHWM) on Linux; does nothing on other systems.
~ (read() can't be used: /proc files report size 0, so the shell reads it for us.)
~ Guarded script: prints "FAIL ..." on a wrong result.

use system

~ 8 throwaway lists of 1,000,000 items (~59 MB each): ~470 MB if never collected.
~ GC stress mode collects on every statement, so there it runs a small version: still a
~ use-after-free check, and peak memory says nothing when everything is collected anyway.
make lists = 8
make items = 1000000
make stress = env("SPROUT_GC_STRESS", "") != ""
when stress:
    set lists = 2
    set items = 20000

when exists("/proc/version"):
    repeat lists times:
        make big = []
        repeat items times:
            add(big, 1)
    make out = trim(system.run("grep VmHWM /proc/$PPID/status"))
    make peak = 0
    when contains(out, "VmHWM"):
        set peak = number(split(trim(split(out, ":")[1]), " ")[0])
    ~ Under AddressSanitizer freed memory sits in ASan's quarantine (256 MB by default), so
    ~ peak memory can't show the frees there either.
    when not stress and env("ASAN_OPTIONS", "") == "":
        when peak == 0:
            show "FAIL could not read VmHWM, got:", out
        when peak > 300000:
            show "FAIL list growth was not collected - peak memory", peak, "kB"
